// motion.js — THE WORKS ON THE MOTION-GRAPHICS PAGE, AND WHAT THEY ARE CALLED
//
// This file is the WORDS and the ORDER: each work's name, and where on the page
// it is shown. The video lives in site/motionassets.js (generated by
// tools/bake_motion.mjs) and is joined to this by `id`. The names are the
// editor's to change, and they are lowercase because every other name on the
// page is.
//
// THE PAGE IS A SHOWCASE OF WORKS, not a breakdown of one film (2026-09-26, by
// instruction). Most of these were cut from "let me be a flower"
// (../VisualAudialExperience), but nothing on the page relates a work to that
// film except the film itself: its tile at the foot, and the timeline under it.
//
// TO ADD A WORK: bake its clip (tools/motion/README.md, "Adding a work"), then
// add one line here with the id you baked it under. Where it goes:
//
//   on: ["top"]      the featured wall, in the order written here -- the longest
//                    and most developed works, so they are what a skim sees
//   on: ["mix"]      the scatter under it, in the order written here (placement
//                    is seeded, so the order is a suggestion, not a row)
//   on: ["flower"]   the flower's run: one pinned frame the scroll steps
//                    through, in this order
//
// A work can be in two places ("mix" and "flower"). Every tile's size follows
// its own clip's length, read from the clip, so nothing here says how big.
//
// SOUND IS THE EXCEPTION (2026-10-03, by instruction): only the works marked
// `sound: true` have an audio track, and the film's own score stays. Every
// other clip is silent, in the file and not just muted, and the viewer shows
// no speaker on it. tools/bake_motion.mjs reads this flag when it cuts a clip
// (a silent one is written with -an), so a re-bake keeps it true; `--add ...
// --sound` is how a new work gets a track. npm test checks each file against it.

